const faq = [
  { q: 'Do all birds play, or is raven play unusual?', a: 'Play behaviour is relatively rare in birds compared to mammals, and concentrated in species with large brains relative to body size — corvids, parrots, and raptors being the most prominent examples. Among corvids, ravens and New Caledonian Crows are considered the most playful. The breadth, complexity, and social nature of raven play is unusual in the bird world.' },
  { q: 'What is object play in ravens?', a: 'Object play involves manipulating items for no immediate feeding purpose. Ravens have been observed carrying twigs, pebbles, wire, and other objects in flight, dropping and catching them, and passing objects to other ravens. In one study, juvenile ravens repeatedly dropped and re-caught objects in flight, an activity that serves no obvious survival function during the play bout itself.' },
  { q: 'Do ravens play with animals other than ravens?', a: 'Yes. Ravens have been documented engaging in play-like interactions with wolves (pulling tails, darting towards and away), dogs, and even other raven species. The wolf–raven play interactions documented at Yellowstone are particularly well-known. These cross-species interactions appear to be initiated by both parties and do not typically end in predation or aggression.' },
];
